commentBody:
@Nicholas Mitchell
Craves isn't supposed to/mean/ "needs no introduction". It is a synonym of "needs" that lacks its first letter, aka an introduction.  

It's cryptic crossword style clue: part ("needs") is a synonym, the rest ("no introduction") describes what to do to the word. It's just that the clue is the result ("_raves") and the answer is the synonym-plus-manipulation.

@SP

Yep.  The theme was wafer thin and the wafers were floppy and moldy.  Really elementary.  I kept looking / hoping for something clever to pull things together - maybe the missing letters also do something? - but apparently it really is just "chop letters from random word and comment on it".  Ick.
  :(

@GD

This is what I mean by lazy.  As far as I know craves it's just being used as a synonym for needs.  And it could have been ANY synonym for needs.  _ANTS or _EQUIRES etc. etc. etc.

Maybe I'm still missing something, but this is a really lazy theme implementation.

@Lewis

IN_

Unfinished business

@Eric

We use IOTA so much around these parts that for a moment I forgot it's a Greek letter. 
And, really, c'mon now,  IOTA know that kind of stuff with no hesitation.

@Helen Wright @Ken S
  Same here.
  It was also interesting to encounter in college a Jewish woman who loved the books and had had no idea of the Christian connection.

@Skeptical1

In addition to its literal meaning of “not (yet) told,” “untold” also means innumerable:

<a href="https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/untold" target="_blank">https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/untold</a>

@Xword Junkie
I guess (C)RAVES makes more sense than how I interpreted it. I came up with just RAVES because when an MC raves about someone, they often say they need no introduction (although introductions are normally provided). Likewise, that word needs no introducing letter (although the _ would normally call for it). 

It would violate the pattern, but in a rather fiendish way.

@Mark P
'Untold' can mean not only 'unrevealed' but also 'uncounted', i.e., too many to count. That could also be A TON OF.
'Striplings' are slender young men or LADS.

@TazTheGreat

I believe that the spelling "Marion" is more often for a male name while "Marian" is more likely for a female name.

Same with "Francis " for a male and "Frances" for a female.

